terial demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating paragraph unity on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: Two or three clear sentences/paragraphs with capitals, stops and a possessive if used; ideas sequenced. Reasoning: I used paragraph unity by attending to topic control rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with paragraph unity, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Comma-splicing whole clauses without a conjunction or stop. Correction: Strong answer: Two or three clear sentences/paragraphs with capitals, stops and a possessive if used; ideas sequenced. Why: the correction restores accurate use of paragraph unity.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y7-w02-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise paragraph unity.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use paragraph unity with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify topic control and explain why it matters.","I can practise punctuation independently on a new prompt.","I can notice cohesion needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Paragraphs in narrative and analytical writing need unity: one main job per paragraph, controlled topic shift, and punctuation that clarifies (commas in lists/clauses, apostrophes for possession, sentence-ending marks). Cohesive ties (then, across the foyer, her second attempt)…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Paragraphs in narrative and analytical writing need unity: one main job per paragraph, controlled topic shift, and punctuation that clarifies (commas in lists/clauses, apostrophes for possession, sentence-ending marks). Cohesive ties (then, across the foyer, her second attempt) guide readers between sentences.","A claim unrelated to the text."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about paragraph unity?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWeak dump: five unrelated facts in one block. Strong: Paragraph 1—blocked door and Mara’s goal. Paragraph 2—side entrance discovery. A semicolon or full stop separates complete ideas; ''Mara’s bag'' uses possessive apostrophe correctly.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSplit and punctuate: mara pushed again the door stayed shut she checked the side gate it swung open\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Two or three clear sentences/paragraphs with capitals, stops and a possessive if used; ideas sequenced.","explain":"Paragraphs in narrative and analytical writing need unity: one main job per paragraph, controlled topic shift, and punctuation that clarifies (commas in lists/clauses, apostrophes for possession, sentence-ending marks). Cohesive ties (then, across the foyer, her second attempt) guide readers between sentences.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Comma-splicing whole clauses without a conjunction or stop. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and